{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/deviant-the-renegades/1481603-deviant-what-variations-would-i-need-to-survive-in-space","id":1481603,"title":"[Deviant] What Variations would I need to survive in space?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Deviant: The Renegades"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-05-21T09:13:05-07:00","id":1481603,"title":"[Deviant] What Variations would I need to survive in space?","content":"I realized that the fastest flight I could get I wouldn't be able to get anywhere. But I still want to know. Can anyone help me?<br /> <br /> Ty","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1383-yrtalien","name":"yrtalien","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"97"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-21T10:25:34-07:00","id":1481608,"title":"","content":"A high rating of <i>Environmental Adaptation</i> should do the trick.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>~<span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\">\u266b</span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7593-teatime","name":"Teatime","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7593_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"750"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-21T11:54:00-07:00","id":1481612,"title":"","content":"Anomalous Biology (Breathless, Hungerless) and Immunity 2 (Radiation, Cold) spring to mind.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-21T12:35:37-07:00","id":1481615,"title":"","content":"Scientific trivia! While technically the void of space is close to absolute zero in temperature, freezing there is not a concern. Why? Because the lack of air makes it act like a thermos. There's little to no matter that you could lose heat to. Now, the almost non-existent pressure there will play merry havoc with your organism, even if you don't need to breathe.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>~<span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\">\u266b</span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7593-teatime","name":"Teatime","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7593_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"750"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-23T15:53:54-07:00","id":1481792,"title":"","content":"Even the lack of pressure won't work so fast, you won't pop like a pimple or something. Exposure to the sun is a problem, but also won't work fast enough. A human body lost in space would probably die from a seemingly mundane lack of oxygen far before any of the more unusual damage. Seemingly, because the lack of pressure actually reverses how the lungs work, robbing your blood of oxygen instead of just not giving you any, but even then you would have from 30 seconds to a full minute of conscience followed by about 1 minute unconscious but alive.<br /> <br /> Also forget about experiences with eggs in bottles and whatnot, without the extremely specific (if not hard to manufacture) circumstances of those experiments, the reality is that even the &quot;extreme&quot; pressure differential between 1atm and the vacuum of space isn't all that strong. Closing a hole in a spaceship hull with your finger would be terrible for your finger, but would actually work fine.<br /> <br /> But yeah, once breathing is taken care of, your main concerns are heat, cold and pressure, but there are ways around. A reflexive surface for the harmful radiation (we're not talking the particulate radiation usually associated with nuclear devices or radioactive materials) would help a lot with the heat, and the same kind of insulation would be needed for the cold, as it would be a matter of keeping infrared emissions in instead of using normal insulation. Without it heat would greatly depend on circumstances of exposure, but would hardly kill in less than some hours, while cold would steadily freeze you in about 36 hours.<br /> <br /> Pressure is interesting. Its most obvious problem isn't a problem at all, but it does causes havoc to your metabolism because many chemical processes change when pressure changes. But at the same time, an organism build around those changes or around the processes if a specific state of pressure isn't particularly hard to exist, such things actually do exist <b><i>in real life</i></b>. Blood doesn't boil, but it can form water vapor bubbles that causes embolism, among other changes, but alternative processes and some inner pressure equalizers aren't terribly hard to come by. If you can engineer a mutation to survive under water and/or in high altitudes, you're almost there for vacuum.<br /> <br /> The last problem would be Solar Winds. <b>those</b> are radioactive particles and can cause radiation sickness. They do work more like weather phenomena, so aren't a constant state, and aren't the most terrible source of radiation exposure either. Recent studies and observations in Chernobyl before the current war showed that most organisms are far more resistant to radioactivity than humans too, to the point that nature mostly recovered to a pre-human state in the exclusion zone instead of mutating and decaying. A Variation able to resist and recover from a solar wind, even a strong one, wouldn't need to be extreme. All in all, most of those wouldn't need too extreme measures even if organic ones.<br /> <br /> Space debris may be a problem from time to time in some areas, but even a perfectly human healing factor would be enough to take care of this.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-23T16:43:27-07:00","id":1481800,"title":"","content":"So the appended answer seems to be &quot;Anomalous Biology 3 (Breathless, Lifeless, Hungerless, [free space]) and Immunity 2 (Radiation).&quot;<br /> <br /> Primary takeaway is &quot;Mutants are good at survival.&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-23T17:14:09-07:00","id":1481805,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/deviant-the-renegades/1481603-deviant-what-variations-would-i-need-to-survive-in-space?p=1481800#post1481800\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So the appended answer seems to be &quot;Anomalous Biology 3 (Breathless, Lifeless, Hungerless, [free space]) and Immunity 2 (Radiation).&quot;<br /> <br /> Primary takeaway is &quot;Mutants are good at survival.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>Too good, actually. I couldn't buy the book yet, so I know not about it rules-wise to be able to point a simpler answer, but it is actually simple enough that this power set seems like overkill for the task at hand. There are far worse conditions on Earth alone than space. Breathing <b>really</b> is the biggest issue.<br /> <br /> Oh, and moving about, of course.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}